helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146041	2			0	tr	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FLD_INPUT\"\>Inserts a text field that you can open and edit by clicking it in the document.\</ahelp\> You can use input fields for text, or to assign a new value to a variable.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154470	7			0	tr	To change the content of an Input Field in a document, click the field, and then edit the text in the lower box of the dialog.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153669	3			0	tr	Düzenle				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154571	4			0	tr	\<ahelp hid=\"SW:MULTILINEEDIT:DLG_FLD_INPUT:ED_EDIT\"\>The upper box displays the name that you entered in the \<emph\>Reference\</emph\> box of the Input Field on the \<emph\>Functions \</emph\>tab of the \<emph\>Fields\</emph\> dialog. The lower box displays the contents of the field.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155897	5			0	tr	Sonraki				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149691	6			0	tr	\<ahelp hid=\"SW:PUSHBUTTON:DLG_FLD_INPUT:PB_NEXT\"\>Jumps to the next input field in the document.\</ahelp\> This button is only available when you position the cursor directly before an input field, and then press Shift+Ctrl+F9.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	tit				0	tr	Defining Conditions				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145828				0	tr	\<bookmark_value\>logical expressions\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>formulating conditions\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>conditions; in fields and sections\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>fields;defining conditions\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>sections;defining conditions\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>variables; in conditions\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>user data;in conditions\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>databases;in conditions\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>hiding; database fields\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145828	1			0	tr	\<variable id=\"defining_conditions\"\>\<link href=\"text/swriter/01/04090200.xhp\"\>Defining Conditions\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145242	13			0	tr	Conditions are logical expressions that you can use to control the display of \<link href=\"text/swriter/01/04090000.xhp\"\>fields\</link\> and \<link href=\"text/swriter/01/04020000.xhp\"\>sections\</link\> in your document. Although the following examples apply to fields, they also apply to sections.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147171	93			0	tr	You can define conditions for the following field types:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151185	14			0	tr	Conditional text: displays text A if the condition is true, or text B if the condition is false.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149289	15			0	tr	Hidden text: hides the contents of the field if the condition is true.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145412	16			0	tr	Hidden paragraph: hides the paragraph if the condition is true.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147515	17			0	tr	Any record and next record: controls the access to database records.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149802	18			0	tr	The simplest way to define a condition is to type the logical expression directly in a \<emph\>Condition \</emph\>box using the following values:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153677	19			0	tr	Doğru				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3152960	20			0	tr	The condition is always met. You can also enter any value not equal to 0 as the conditional text.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155900	21			0	tr	Yanlış				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154191	22			0	tr	The condition is not met. You can also enter the value 0.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147090	23			0	tr	If you leave the \<emph\>Condition \</emph\>box empty, the condition is interpreted as not being met.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148980	24			0	tr	When you define a condition, use the same \<link href=\"text/swriter/02/14020000.xhp\"\>elements\</link\> for defining a formula, namely comparative operators, mathematical and statistical functions, number formats, variables and constants.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153638	25			0	tr	You can use the following types of variables when you define a condition:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155135	27			0	tr	Predefined \<link href=\"text/swriter/02/14020000.xhp\"\>$[officename] variables\</link\> that use statistics on document properties				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156273	26			0	tr	Custom variables, that are a created with the "Set variable" field				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149174	94			0	tr	Kullanıcı verisindeki temel değişkenler				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145781	28			0	tr	Veritabanı alanı içeriğindeki temel değişkenler				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155916	29			0	tr	You cannot use internal variables, such as page and chapter numbers, in condition expression.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151375	30			0	tr	Koşullar ve Değişkenler				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150122	31			0	tr	The following examples use a variable called "x":				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147417	32			0	tr	x == 1 or x EQ 1				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147500	33			0	tr	The condition is true if "x" is equal to 1.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145259	34			0	tr	x != 1 or x NEQ 1				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150551	35			0	tr	The condition is true if "x" does not equal 1.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147749	36			0	tr	sinx == 0				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3146345	37			0	tr	The condition is true if "x" is a multiple of pi.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149846	38			0	tr	To use comparative operators with strings, the operands must be bounded by double quotation marks:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151078	39			0	tr	x == "ABC" or x EQ "ABC"				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150162	40			0	tr	Checks if variable "x" contains (true) the "ABC" string, or not (false).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153301	41			0	tr	x == "" or x EQ ""				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156120	90			0	tr	VEYA				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156133	42			0	tr	!x or NOT x				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156112	44			0	tr	Checks if the variable "x" contains an empty string.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150097	45			0	tr	The "equal" comparative operator must be represented by two equal signs (==) in a condition. For example, if you define a variable "x" with the value of 1, you can enter the condition as x==1.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148791	95			0	tr	Kullanıcı Verisi				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150028	96			0	tr	You can include user data when you define conditions. To change your user data, choose \<emph\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - $[officename] - User data\</emph\>. User data must be entered in the form of strings. You can query the user data with "==" (EQ), "!=" (NEQ), or "!"(NOT).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153124	97			0	tr	The following table lists user data variables and their meanings:				20130618 17:22:18
