helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154657	2			0	tr	How do you align characters in $[officename] Math quickly and easily?				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	par_id3150249	3			0	tr	To accomplish this, you must define empty groups and character strings. They do not require any space, but carry information that helps in the alignment process.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	par_id3153912	4			0	tr	To create empty groups, enter curly brackets \<emph\>{}\</emph\> in the Commands window. In the following example, the goal is to achieve a line break so that the plus signs are beneath one another, even though one less character is entered in the upper line:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	par_id3153246	7			0	tr	a+a+a+{} newline {}{}{}{}{}a+a+a+a				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	par_id3143229	5			0	tr	Empty character strings are a simple way to ensure that texts and formulas are left-aligned. They are defined using double inverted commas "" . Make sure you do not use any typographic inverted commas. Example:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	par_id3153809	6			0	tr	"A further example." newline a+b newline ""c-d				20130618 17:22:18
