helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154484	2			0	tr	$[officename] Math'da varsayılan biçimleri değiştirebilir miyim?				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	par_id3148870	3			0	tr	Some parts of formulas are always formatted bold or italic by default.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	par_id3150210	7			0	tr	You can remove these attributes using "nbold" and "nitalic". Example:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	par_id3143231	6			0	tr	a + b				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	par_id3153810	5			0	tr	nitalic a + bold b.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	par_id3149872	4			0	tr	In the second formula, the a is not italic. The b is bold. You cannot change the plus sign by this method.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	tit				0	tr	Merging Formula Parts in Brackets				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	bm_id3152596				0	tr	\<bookmark_value\>brackets; merging formula parts\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formula parts; merging\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>fractions in formulas\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>merging;formula parts\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152596	1			0	tr	\<variable id=\"brackets\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/brackets.xhp\" name=\"Merging Formula Parts in Brackets\"\>Merging Formula Parts in Brackets\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154511	2			0	tr	Inserting fractions into formulas				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	par_id3146971	4			0	tr	In the case of a fraction whose numerator and denominator consist of a product, a sum, and so on, the values that belong together must be bracketed together.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	par_id3149021	3			0	tr	Sonraki sözdizimini kullan:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	par_id3083280	7			0	tr	{a + c} over 2 = m				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	par_id3154703	6			0	tr	VEYA				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	par_id3150297	5			0	tr	m = {a + c} over 2				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\comment.xhp	0	help	tit				0	tr	Açıklama Girişi				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\comment.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155961				0	tr	\<bookmark_value\>comments; entering in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inserting;comments in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\comment.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155961	1			0	tr	\<variable id=\"comment\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/comment.xhp\" name=\"Entering Comments\"\>Açıklama Girişi\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\comment.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154657	2			0	tr	How does one attach comments that don't appear in the document to a formula?				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\comment.xhp	0	help	par_id3149499	3			0	tr	A comment begins with a double percent sign \<emph\>%%\</emph\>, and extends to the next line-end character (Enter key). Everything that lies in between is ignored and is not printed out. If there are percent signs in the text, they are treated as part of the text.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\comment.xhp	0	help	par_idN105D0				0	tr	Örnek:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\comment.xhp	0	help	par_idN105D3				0	tr	a^2+b^2=c^2 %% Pisagor Teoremi.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	tit				0	tr	Kısayollar ($[officename] Math Erişebilirlik)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149018				0	tr	\<bookmark_value\>erişebilirlik; $[officename] Math kısayolları\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149018	12			0	tr	\<variable id=\"keyboard\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/keyboard.xhp\" name=\"Shortcuts ($[officename] Math Accessibility)\"\>Kısayollar ($[officename] Math Erişebilirlik)\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3150298	1			0	tr	$[officename] Math'i fare ile kontrol edebilirsiniz.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150343	2			0	tr	Doğrudan Formül Ekleme				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3154659	3			0	tr	Eğer metin belgesine bir formül eklemek isterseniz ve önceden nasıl yazacağını biliyorsanız, aşağıdaki gibi devam edebilirsiniz:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3153818	4			0	tr	Metniniz içine formül yazmak				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3153915	5			0	tr	Formülü seçin				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3150213	6			0	tr	\<emph\>Ekle - Nesne - Formül\</emph\> komutunu seçin.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154767	7			0	tr	Pencere kullanarak Formül ekleme				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3149875	8			0	tr	If you want to use the $[officename] Math interface to edit a formula, choose the command \<emph\>Insert - Object - Formula\</emph\> without any text selected.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3150391	9			0	tr	The cursor waits in the Commands window and you can type the formula.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3150537	13			0	tr	You can compose formulas using the Elements window. Open it with the menu \<emph\>View - Elements\</emph\> if it is not already open.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3155625	10			0	tr	If the Elements window is open, use F6 to switch from the Commands window to the Elements window and back.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154554	11			0	tr	Elements window				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	tit				0	tr	Working with Limits				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	bm_id8404492				0	tr	\<bookmark_value\>limits;in sums/integrals\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>integral limits\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	hd_id1892147				0	tr	\<variable id=\"limits\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/limits.xhp\"\>Working with Limits\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	hd_id9881893				0	tr	How can I define the limits in a Sum or Integral formula?				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id6504409				0	tr	You want to insert a summation formula like "summation of s^k from k = 0 to n" at the cursor in a Writer text document.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id8811304				0	tr	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Insert - Object - Formula\</item\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id1276589				0	tr	You see the Math input window and the Elements window. If you don't see the Elements window, you can enable it in the View menu.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id3283791				0	tr	In the upper part of the Elements window, click the \<emph\>Operators\</emph\> icon.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id9734794				0	tr	In the lower part of the Elements window, click the \<emph\>Sum\</emph\> icon.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id9641712				0	tr	To enable lower and upper limits, click additionally the \<emph\>Upper and Lower Limits\</emph\> icon.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id3304239				0	tr	In the input window, the first placeholder or marker is selected, and you can start to enter the lower limit: 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id8679158				0	tr	k = 0				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id8471327				0	tr	Press F4 to advance to the next marker, and enter the upper limit:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id4731894				0	tr	n				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id759300				0	tr	Press F4 to advance to the next marker, and enter the summand:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id651875				0	tr	s^k				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id6756187				0	tr	Now the formula is complete. Click into your text document outside the formula to leave the formula editor.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id9406414				0	tr	In the same way, you can enter an Integral formula with limits. When you click an icon from the Elements window, the assigned text command is inserted in the input window. If you know the text commands, you can enter the commands directly in the input window.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id5866267				0	tr	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Insert - Object - Formula\</item\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id1965697				0	tr	Click in the input window and enter the following line:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id9004486				0	tr	int from{a} to{b} f(x)`dx				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id4651020				0	tr	A small gap exists between f(x) and dx, which you can also enter using the Elements window: click the \<emph\>Format\</emph\> icon, then the \<emph\>Small Gap\</emph\> icon.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id3877071				0	tr	If you don't like the font of the letters f and x, choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format - Fonts\</item\> and select other fonts. Click the \<emph\>Default\</emph\> button to use the new fonts as default from now on.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id3021332				0	tr	If you need the formula within a line of text, the limits increase the line height. You can choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format - Text Mode\</item\> to place the limits besides the Sum or Integral symbol, which reduces the line height.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id260322				0	tr	\<link href=\"text/smath/01/03090909.xhp\"\>Example of Integral and Sum ranges\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
