helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_id3155394	48			0	tr	For alignment, the \<emph\>alignl, alignc\</emph\> and \<emph\>alignr\</emph\> commands are especially effective, if you are 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_id3151009	49			0	tr	aligning numerators and denominators, for example \<emph\>{alignl a}over{b+c}\</emph\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_id3148812	50			0	tr	constructing binomials or stacks, for example \<emph\>binom{2*n}{alignr k}\</emph\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_id3154360	51			0	tr	aligning the elements in a matrix, for example \<emph\>matrix{alignr a#b+2##c+1/3#alignl d}\</emph\> and				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_id3155946	52			0	tr	beginning a new line, for example \<emph\>a+b-c newline alignr x/y\</emph\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_id3154621	53			0	tr	When using the align instructions, note that				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_id3147382	54			0	tr	they can only placed at the beginning of expressions and can only occur once. Therefore you can type \<emph\>a+b alignr c\</emph\>, but not \<emph\>a+alignr b\</emph\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_id3154004	55			0	tr	they affect each other, which means that typing \<emph\>{alignl{alignr a}}over{b+c}\</emph\> aligns \<emph\>a\</emph\> on the right.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	hd_id8036133				0	tr	To align using the "matrix" command				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_idN10F70				0	tr	Aligning to the left				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_id3149645	56			0	tr	If a line or an expression begins with text, it is aligned on the left by default. You can change this with any of the \<emph\>align\</emph\> commands. An example is \<emph\>stack{a+b-c*d#alignr "text"}\</emph\>, where "text" appears aligned to the right. Note that text must always be surrounded by quotation marks.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_id3149966	57			0	tr	The standard centralized formulas can be aligned to the left without using the \<emph\>Format - Alignment\</emph\> menu. To do this, place an empty character string, that is, the inverted commas which surround any text "", before the section of formula that you want to align. For example, typing \<emph\>"" a+b newline "" c+d\</emph\> results in both equations being left-aligned instead of centered.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_id3145654	46			0	tr	When typing information in the Commands window, note that some formats require spaces for the correct structure. This is especially true when entering values (for example, a lsup{3}) instead of placeholders.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_id3148708	47			0	tr	Click \<link href=\"text/smath/01/03091100.xhp\" name=\"Brackets and Grouping\"\>Brackets and Grouping\</link\> for more information about formatting in \<emph\>$[officename] Math\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_id3155340	31			0	tr	Useful information about \<link href=\"text/smath/01/03091200.xhp\" name=\"Indexes and Exponents\"\>Indexes and Exponents\</link\> and \<link href=\"text/smath/01/03091400.xhp\" name=\"Scaling\"\>Scaling\</link\>, helps you organize your document in the best possible way.				20130618 17:22:18
