helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	tit				0	tr	Arranging, Aligning and Distributing Objects				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149656	73			0	tr	\<variable id=\"align_arrange\"\>\<link href=\"text/sdraw/guide/align_arrange.xhp\" name=\"Arranging and Aligning Objects\"\>Arranging, Aligning and Distributing Objects\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	bm_id3125863				0	tr	\<bookmark_value\>arranging; objects (guide)\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>objects;aligning\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>distributing draw objects\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>aligning;draw objects\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	hd_id3125863	17			0	tr	Arranging Objects				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_id3153727	18			0	tr	Each object that you place in your document is successively stacked on the preceding object. To re-arrange the stacking order of a selected object, proceed as follows.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_idN107D5				0	tr	Click the object whose position you want to change.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_id3150327	78			0	tr	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Modify - Arrange\</item\> to bring up the context menu and choose one of the arrange options:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_idN107E6				0	tr	\<emph\>Bring to Front\</emph\> places the object on top of all other objects				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_idN107EC				0	tr	\<emph\>Bring Forward\</emph\> places the object one place forward in the stack of objects				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_idN107F2				0	tr	\<emph\>Send Backward\</emph\> places the object one place back in the stack of objects				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_idN107F8				0	tr	\<emph\>Send to Back\</emph\> places the object behind all other objects				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_idN107FE				0	tr	\<emph\>Behind Object\</emph\> places the object behind another object that you select				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155766	79			0	tr	Arranging an Object Behind Another Object				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_idN10811				0	tr	Click the object whose position you want to change.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_id3154253	80			0	tr	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Modify - Arrange\</item\> to open the context menu and choose \<emph\>Behind Object\</emph\>. The mouse pointer changes to a hand.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_id3149126	81			0	tr	Click the object behind which you want to place the selected object.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145789	20			0	tr	Reversing The Stacking Order of Two Objects				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_id3154022	83			0	tr	Shift-click both objects to select them.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_id3155114	84			0	tr	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Modify - Arrange\</item\> to open the context menu and choose \<emph\>Reverse\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	hd_id3166425	21			0	tr	Aligning Objects				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_id3152994	22			0	tr	The \<emph\>Alignment\</emph\> function enables you to align objects relative to each other or relative to the page.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_idN108A3				0	tr	Select an object to align it to the page or select multiple objects to align them relative to each other.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_idN108A7				0	tr	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Modify - Alignment\</item\> and select one of the alignment options.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_idN108AE				0	tr	Distributing Objects				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_id3151390	71			0	tr	If you select three or more objects in Draw, you can also use the \<link href=\"text/shared/01/05360000.xhp\" name=\"Distribution\"\>\<emph\>Distribution\</emph\>\</link\> command to distribute the vertical and horizontal spacing evenly between the objects.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_idN108CE				0	tr	Select three or more objects to be distributed.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_idN108D2				0	tr	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Modify - Distribution\</item\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_idN108DA				0	tr	Select the horizontal and vertical distribution option and click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_id3150535	72			0	tr	Selected objects are distributed evenly along the horizontal or vertical axis. The two outermost objects are used as reference points and do not move when the \<emph\>Distribution\</emph\> command is applied.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	tit				0	tr	Defining Custom Colors				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149263				0	tr	\<bookmark_value\>colors; defining and saving\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>user-defined colors\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>custom colors\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149263	7			0	tr	\<variable id=\"color_define\"\>\<link href=\"text/sdraw/guide/color_define.xhp\" name=\"Defining Custom Colors\"\>Defining Custom Colors\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3154511	8			0	tr	If you want, you can mix a custom color and add it to a color table.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155600	9			0	tr	To define a custom color				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3150327	25			0	tr	Choose \<emph\>Format - Area\</emph\> and click the \<emph\>Colors\</emph\> tab. A table of the predefined colors is displayed.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3154657	13			0	tr	Changes made to the standard color table are permanent and are saved automatically.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3166425	14			0	tr	Click a color in the table that is similar to the one you want to mix. The color appears in the upper preview box to the right of the table.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3152992	15			0	tr	Select the RGB or CMYK color model in the box below the preview boxes.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id4979705				0	tr	%PRODUCTNAME uses only the RGB color model for printing in color. The CMYK controls are provided only to ease the input of color values using CMYK notation.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3152987	16			0	tr	The RGB color model mixes red, green and blue light to create colors on a computer screen. In the RGB model, the three color components are additive and can have values ranging from 0 (black) to 255 (white). The CMYK color model combines Cyan (C), Magenta (M), Yellow (Y), and blacK (K, also used for "Key") to create colors for printing. The four colors of the CMYK models are subtractive and are defined as percentages. Black corresponds to 100 % and white to 0 %.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3145386	17			0	tr	Enter a numeric value in the boxes next to the color components. The new color appears in the preview box directly above the color model box.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3152871	18			0	tr	You can also create a color using a color spectrum. Click the \<emph\>Edit\</emph\> button to open the \<link href=\"text/shared/optionen/01010501.xhp\" name=\"Color\"\>\<emph\>Color\</emph\>\</link\> dialog. Click a color. Use the Hue, Saturation, and Brightness boxes to adjust your color selection.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3153011	19			0	tr	Do one of the following:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3147244	26			0	tr	If you want to replace the color in the standard color table that your custom color is based on, click \<emph\>Modify\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3145116	20			0	tr	If you want to add your custom color to the standard color table, enter a name in the \<emph\>Name\</emph\> text box and click \<emph\>Add\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3145236	23			0	tr	\<link href=\"text/shared/01/03170000.xhp\" name=\"Color bar\"\>Color bar\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	tit				0	tr	Combining Objects and Constructing Shapes				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	bm_id3156443				0	tr	\<bookmark_value\>combining; draw objects\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>merging; draw objects\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>connecting; draw objects\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>draw objects; combining\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>intersecting draw objects\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>polygons; intersecting/subtracting/merging\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>subtracting polygons\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>constructing shapes\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156443	64			0	tr	\<variable id=\"combine_etc\"\>\<link href=\"text/sdraw/guide/combine_etc.xhp\" name=\"Combining Objects and Constructing Shapes\"\>Combining Objects and Constructing Shapes\</link\> \</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3149020	65			0	tr	Combined drawing objects act as grouped objects, except that you cannot enter the group to edit the individual objects. 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3154659	87			0	tr	You can only combine 2D objects.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150344	32			0	tr	To combine 2D objects:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3166428	66			0	tr	Select two or more 2D objects.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3145587	67			0	tr	Choose \<emph\>Modify - Combine\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3146978	33			0	tr	Unlike groups, a combined object takes on the properties of the lowermost object in the stacking order. You can split apart combined objects, but the original object properties are lost.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3155088	34			0	tr	When you combine objects, holes appear where the objects overlap.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3156019				0	tr	 \<image id=\"img_id3157978\" src=\"res/helpimg/kombi1.png\" width=\"8.3646inch\" height=\"2.5inch\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3157978\"\>Illustration for combining objects\</alt\>\</image\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3153249	35			0	tr	In the illustration, the uncombined objects are on the left and the combined objects on the right.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159229	68			0	tr	Constructing Shapes				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3150049	63			0	tr	You can construct shapes by applying the \<link href=\"text/simpress/01/13180000.xhp\" name=\"Shapes\"\>\<emph\>Shapes\</emph\>\</link\> \<emph\>- Merge, Subtract and Intersect\</emph\> commands to two or more drawing objects.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3147403	88			0	tr	Shape commands only work on 2D objects.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3150539	89			0	tr	Constructed shapes take on the properties of the lowermost object in the stacking order.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156402	90			0	tr	To construct a shape:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3157874	69			0	tr	Select two or more 2D objects.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3150650	70			0	tr	Choose \<emph\>Modify - Shapes\</emph\> and one of the following:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3145829	91			0	tr	 \<emph\>Merge\</emph\> 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3154680	92			0	tr	 \<emph\>Subtract\</emph\> 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3153034	93			0	tr	 \<emph\>Intersect\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145144	94			0	tr	Shape Commands				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3153931	71			0	tr	In the following illustrations, the original objects are on the left and the modified shapes on the right.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149950	72			0	tr	Shapes - Merge				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3148585				0	tr	 \<image id=\"img_id3145593\" src=\"res/helpimg/formvers.png\" width=\"3.4791inch\" height=\"1.3126inch\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3145593\"\>Illustration for merging shapes\</alt\>\</image\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3150001	73			0	tr	Adds the area of the selected objects to the area of the lowermost object in the stacking order.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153002	74			0	tr	Shapes - Subtract				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3150338				0	tr	 \<image id=\"img_id3154505\" src=\"res/helpimg/formsubt.png\" width=\"3.4689inch\" height=\"1.3126inch\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3154505\"\>Illustration for subtracting shapes\</alt\>\</image\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3150022	75			0	tr	Subtracts the area of the selected objects from the area of the lowermost object in the stacking order.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147370	78			0	tr	Shapes - Intersect				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3150570				0	tr	 \<image id=\"img_id3150658\" src=\"res/helpimg/formschn.png\" width=\"3.4272inch\" height=\"1.302inch\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3150658\"\>Illustration for intersecting shapes\</alt\>\</image\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3157972	79			0	tr	The overlapping area of the selected objects creates the new shape.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3151020	80			0	tr	The area outside the overlap is removed.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	tit				0	tr	Cross-Fading Two Objects				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150715				0	tr	\<bookmark_value\>draw objects; cross-fading two objects\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cross-fading; two draw objects\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150715	17			0	tr	\<variable id=\"cross_fading\"\>\<link href=\"text/sdraw/guide/cross_fading.xhp\" name=\"Cross-Fading Two Objects\"\>Cross-Fading Two Objects\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	par_id3154754	18			0	tr	Cross-fading creates shapes and distributes them by uniform increments between two drawing objects.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	par_id3155112	41			0	tr	The cross-fading command is only available in $[officename] Draw. You can, however, copy and paste cross-faded objects into $[officename] Impress.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149209	20			0	tr	To cross-fade two objects:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	par_id3150370	45			0	tr	Hold down Shift and click each object.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	par_id3166428	22			0	tr	Choose \<emph\>Edit - Cross-fading\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	par_id3156450	44			0	tr	Enter a value to specify the number of objects between the start and end of the cross-fade in the \<emph\>Increments\</emph\> box.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	par_id3149405	23			0	tr	Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	par_id3151240	24			0	tr	A group containing the two original objects and the specified number (increments) of cross-faded objects is displayed.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	par_id3159203				0	tr	\<image id=\"img_id3150210\" src=\"res/helpimg/ueberblenden.png\" width=\"74.88mm\" height=\"65.62mm\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3150210\"\>Illustration for crossfading\</alt\>\</image\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	par_id3154766	25			0	tr	You can edit the individual objects of a group by selecting the group and pressing F3. Press \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+F3 to exit the group editing mode.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	par_id3155760	42			0	tr	\<link href=\"text/simpress/01/02150000.xhp\" name=\"Editing - Cross-fading\"\>Editing - Cross-fading\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\draw_sector.xhp	0	help	tit				0	tr	Drawing Sectors and Segments				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\draw_sector.xhp	0	help	bm_id3146974				0	tr	\<bookmark_value\>sectors of circles/ellipses\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>segments of circles/ellipses\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>circle segments\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>ellipses; segments\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>drawing; sectors and segments\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\draw_sector.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146974	30			0	tr	\<variable id=\"draw_sector\"\>\<link href=\"text/sdraw/guide/draw_sector.xhp\" name=\"Drawing Sectors and Segments\"\>Drawing Sectors and Segments\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\draw_sector.xhp	0	help	par_id3147396	31			0	tr	The \<emph\>Ellipse\</emph\> toolbar contains tools for drawing ellipses and circles. You can also draw segments and sectors of circles and ellipses.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\draw_sector.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151075	32			0	tr	To draw a sector of a circle or an ellipse:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\draw_sector.xhp	0	help	par_id3155335	33			0	tr	Open the \<emph\>Ellipses\</emph\> toolbar and click one of the \<emph\>Circle Pie\</emph\> or \<emph\>Ellipse Pie\</emph\> icons \<image id=\"img_id3155768\" src=\"res/commandimagelist/sc_circlepie_unfilled.png\" width=\"5.64mm\" height=\"5.64mm\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3155768\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\>. The mouse pointer changes to a cross hair with a small icon of a sector.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\draw_sector.xhp	0	help	par_id3150199	34			0	tr	Position the pointer at the edge of the circle you want to draw and drag to create the circle.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\draw_sector.xhp	0	help	par_id3148868	35			0	tr	To create a circle by dragging from the center, press \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Option\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Alt\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> while dragging.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\draw_sector.xhp	0	help	par_id3145361	36			0	tr	Release the mouse button when the circle has reached the size you want. A line corresponding to the circle radius appears in the circle.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\draw_sector.xhp	0	help	par_id3149872	37			0	tr	Position the pointer where you want to place the first boundary of the sector and click.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\draw_sector.xhp	0	help	par_id3157871	51			0	tr	As the radius line that follows the pointer is constrained to the circle boundaries, you can click anywhere in the document.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\draw_sector.xhp	0	help	par_id3146874	38			0	tr	Position the pointer where you want to place the second boundary of the sector and click. The completed sector is displayed.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\draw_sector.xhp	0	help	par_id3148581	41			0	tr	To draw a segment of a circle or ellipse, follow the steps for creating a sector based on a circle.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\draw_sector.xhp	0	help	par_id3153084	42			0	tr	To draw an arc based on an ellipse, choose one of the arc icons and follow the same steps for creating a sector based on a circle.				20130618 17:22:18
