helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150330	129			0	tr	Geçersiz, özel karakterler kullanılamaz				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155443	13			0	tr	5Numaram=12				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154254	130			0	tr	Geçersiz, değişken bir sayı ile başlayamaz				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147345	16			0	tr	Number,Mine=12				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149256	131			0	tr	Not valid, punctuation marks are not allowed				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146317	17			0	tr	Değişken Tanımlamaları				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150299	18			0	tr	In $[officename] Basic you don't need to declare variables explicitly. A variable declaration can be performed with the \<emph\>Dim\</emph\> statement. You can declare more than one variable at a time by separating the names with a comma. To define the variable type, use either a type-declaration sign after the name, or the appropriate key word. 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154118	140			0	tr	Değişken tanımlamaları için örnekler:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150090	19			0	tr	DIM a$				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150982	132			0	tr	"a" değişkenini bir Dizi olarak tanımlar				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149531	20			0	tr	DIM a As String				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150343	133			0	tr	"a" değişkenini bir Dizi olarak tanımlar				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149036	21			0	tr	DIM a$, b As Integer				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155507	22			0	tr	Bir değişkeni Dizi,bir değişkeni de Tamsayı olarak tanımlar				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_idN10854				0	tr	DIM c As Boolean				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_idN10859				0	tr	Declares c as a Boolean variable that can be TRUE or FALSE				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150519	23			0	tr	It is very important when declaring variables that you use the type-declaration character each time, even if it was used in the declaration instead of a keyword. Thus the following statements are invalid:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152985	24			0	tr	DIM a$				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154527	134			0	tr	"a"'yı bir Dizi olarak tanımlar				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148599	25			0	tr	a="TestString"				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153064	135			0	tr	Type-declaration missing: "a$="				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3144770	26			0	tr	Once you have declared a variable as a certain type, you cannot declare the variable under the same name again as a different type!				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149331	27			0	tr	Değişken Tanımlarının Zorunlu Kılınması				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149443	28			0	tr	Değişkenlerin tanımlanmasını zorunlu kılmak için, aşağıdaki komutu kullanın:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152869	29			0	tr	OPTION EXPLICIT				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155072	30			0	tr	The \<emph\>Option Explicit\</emph\> statement has to be the first line in the module, before the first SUB. Generally, only arrays need to be declared explicitly. All other variables are declared according to the type-declaration character, or - if omitted - as the default type \<emph\>Single\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154614	34			0	tr	Değişken Tipleri				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155383	35			0	tr	$[officename] Basic dört değişken sınıfını destekler:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153972	36			0	tr	\<emph\>Numeric\</emph\> variables can contain number values. Some variables are used to store large or small numbers, and others are used for floating-point or fractional numbers. 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3159226	37			0	tr	\<emph\>String\</emph\> variables contain character strings.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145217	38			0	tr	\<emph\>Boolean\</emph\> variables contain either the TRUE or the FALSE value.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154762	39			0	tr	\<emph\>Object\</emph\> variables can store objects of various types, like tables and documents within a document.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153805	40			0	tr	Tam Sayı Değişkenleri				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146966	41			0	tr	Integer variables range from -32768 to 32767. If you assign a floating-point value to an integer variable, the decimal places are rounded to the next integer. Integer variables are rapidly calculated in procedures and are suitable for counter variables in loops. An integer variable only requires two bytes of memory. "%" is the type-declaration character.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153810	43			0	tr	Dim Variable%				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153556	44			0	tr	Dim Variable As Integer				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147546	45			0	tr	Uzun Tam Sayı Değişkenleri				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151193	46			0	tr	Long integer variables range from -2147483648 to 2147483647. If you assign a floating-point value to a long integer variable, the decimal places are rounded to the next integer. Long integer variables are rapidly calculated in procedures and are suitable for counter variables in loops for large values. A long integer variable requires four bytes of memory. "&" is the type-declaration character.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154708	48			0	tr	Dim Variable&				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156365	49			0	tr	Dim Variable as Long				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id7596972				0	tr	Decimal Variables				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id2649311				0	tr	Decimal variables can take positive or negative numbers or zero. Accuracy is up to 29 digits.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id7617114				0	tr	You can use plus (+) or minus (-) signs as prefixes for decimal numbers (with or without spaces).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id1593676				0	tr	If a decimal number is assigned to an integer variable, %PRODUCTNAME Basic rounds the figure up or down.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147500	50			0	tr	Tek Değişkenleri				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153070	51			0	tr	Single variables can take positive or negative values ranging from 3.402823 x 10E38 to 1.401298 x 10E-45. Single variables are floating-point variables, in which the decimal precision decreases as the non-decimal part of the number increases. Single variables are suitable for mathematical calculations of average precision. Calculations require more time than for Integer variables, but are faster than calculations with Double variables. A Single variable requires 4 bytes of memory. The type-declaration character is "!".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149875	52			0	tr	Dim Variable!				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153302	53			0	tr	Dim Variable as Single				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155753	54			0	tr	Çift Değişkenleri				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150953	55			0	tr	Double variables can take positive or negative values ranging from 1.79769313486232 x 10E308 to 4.94065645841247 x 10E-324. Double variables are floating-point variables, in which the decimal precision decreases as the non-decimal part of the number increases. Double variables are suitable for precise calculations. Calculations require more time than for Single variables. A Double variable requires 8 bytes of memory. The type-declaration character is "#".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150431	56			0	tr	Dim Variable#				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154406	57			0	tr	Dim Variable As Double				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155747	95			0	tr	Para Birimi Değişkenleri				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153337	96			0	tr	Currency variables are internally stored as 64-bit numbers (8 Bytes) and displayed as a fixed-decimal number with 15 non-decimal and 4 decimal places. The values range from -922337203685477.5808 to +922337203685477.5807. Currency variables are used to calculate currency values with a high precision. The type-declaration character is "@".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147296	97			0	tr	Dim Variable@				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150391	98			0	tr	Dim Variable As Currency				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148742	58			0	tr	Dizi Değişkenleri				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151393	59			0	tr	String variables can hold character strings with up to 65,535 characters. Each character is stored as the corresponding Unicode value. String variables are suitable for word processing within programs and for temporary storage of any non-printable character up to a maximum length of 64 Kbytes. The memory required for storing string variables depends on the number of characters in the variable. The type-declaration character is "$".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3166467	60			0	tr	Dim Variable$				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153027	61			0	tr	Dim Variable As String				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150534	62			0	tr	Mantıksal Değişkenler				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145632	63			0	tr	Boolean variables store only one of two values: TRUE or FALSE. A number 0 evaluates to FALSE, every other value evaluates to TRUE.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147615	64			0	tr	Dim Variable As Boolean				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149722	65			0	tr	Tarih Değişkenleri				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3159116	66			0	tr	Date variables can only contain dates and time values stored in an internal format. Values assigned to Date variables with \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030101.xhp\" name=\"Dateserial\"\>\<emph\>Dateserial\</emph\>\</link\>, \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030102.xhp\" name=\"Datevalue\"\>\<emph\>Datevalue\</emph\>\</link\>, \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030205.xhp\" name=\"Timeserial\"\>\<emph\>Timeserial\</emph\>\</link\> or \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030206.xhp\" name=\"Timevalue\"\>\<emph\>Timevalue\</emph\>\</link\> are automatically converted to the internal format. Date-variables are converted to normal numbers by using the \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030103.xhp\" name=\"Day\"\>\<emph\>Day\</emph\>\</link\>, \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030104.xhp\" name=\"Month\"\>\<emph\>Month\</emph\>\</link\>, \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030106.xhp\" name=\"Year\"\>\<emph\>Year\</emph\>\</link\> or the \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030201.xhp\" name=\"Hour\"\>\<emph\>Hour\</emph\>\</link\>, \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030202.xhp\" name=\"Minute\"\>\<emph\>Minute\</emph\>\</link\>, \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030204.xhp\" name=\"Second\"\>\<emph\>Second\</emph\>\</link\> function. The internal format enables a comparison of date/time values by calculating the difference between two numbers. These variables can only be declared with the key word \<emph\>Date\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150462	67			0	tr	Dim Variable As Date				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148732	68			0	tr	Initial Variable Values				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154549	69			0	tr	As soon as the variable has been declared, it is automatically set to the "Null" value. Note the following conventions:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3143222	70			0	tr	\<emph\>Numeric\</emph\> variables are automatically assigned the value "0" as soon as they are declared.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150693	71			0	tr	\<emph\>Date variables\</emph\> are assigned the value 0 internally; equivalent to converting the value to "0" with the \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030103.xhp\" name=\"Day\"\>\<emph\>Day\</emph\>\</link\>, \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030104.xhp\" name=\"Month\"\>\<emph\>Month\</emph\>\</link\>, \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030106.xhp\" name=\"Year\"\>\<emph\>Year\</emph\>\</link\> or the \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030201.xhp\" name=\"Hour\"\>\<emph\>Hour\</emph\>\</link\>, \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030202.xhp\" name=\"Minute\"\>\<emph\>Minute\</emph\>\</link\>, \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030204.xhp\" name=\"Second\"\>\<emph\>Second\</emph\>\</link\> function.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154807	72			0	tr	\<emph\>String variables\</emph\> are assigned an empty-string ("") when they are declared.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153936	83			0	tr	Diziler				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148736	84			0	tr	$[officename] Basic knows one- or multi-dimensional arrays, defined by a specified variable type. Arrays are suitable for editing lists and tables in programs. Individual elements of an array can be addressed through a numeric index.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149546	85			0	tr	Arrays \<emph\>must\</emph\> be declared with the \<emph\>Dim\</emph\> statement. There are several ways to define the index range of an array:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150143	86			0	tr	DIM text$(20)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154567	136			0	tr	21 eleman 0'dan 20'ye kadar numaralandı				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145596	125			0	tr	DIM text$(5,4)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154397	137			0	tr	30 eleman (6 x 5 lik bir matris elemanları)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149185	87			0	tr	DIM text$(5 to 25)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149690	138			0	tr	21 eleman 5'ten 25'e kadar numaralandı				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155950	88			0	tr	DIM text$(-15 to 5)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153113	89			0	tr	21 eleman (0 dahil), -15'ten 5'e kadar numaralandı				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153005	90			0	tr	Dizin aralığı, pozitif ve negatif sayılar içerebilir				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154507	91			0	tr	Sabitler				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156357	92			0	tr	Constants have a fixed value. They are only defined once in the program and cannot be redefined later:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153203	93			0	tr	CONST ConstName=Expression				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020200.xhp	0	help	tit				0	tr	Nesneler Kullanılır				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145645	1			0	tr	\<variable id=\"01020200\"\>\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01020200.xhp\"\>Using the Object Catalog\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153707	76			0	tr	The object catalog provides an overview of all modules and dialogs you have created in $[officename].				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147346	78			0	tr	Click the \<emph\>Object Catalog\</emph\> icon \<image id=\"img_id3147341\" src=\"res/commandimagelist/sc_objectcatalog.png\" width=\"0.564cm\" height=\"0.564cm\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3147341\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\> in the Macro toolbar to display the object catalog.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155114	79			0	tr	The dialog shows a list of all existing objects in a hierarchical representation. Double-clicking a list entry opens its subordinate objects.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150786	83			0	tr	To display a certain module in the Editor or to position the cursor in a selected SUB or FUNCTION, select the corresponding entry and click the \<emph\>Show\</emph\> icon \<image id=\"img_id3149527\" src=\"basctl/res/im01.png\" width=\"0.564cm\" height=\"0.564cm\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3149527\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153266	81			0	tr	Nesne kataloğunu kapatmak için başlık çubuğundaki (X) simgesine tıklayınız.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	tit				0	tr	Yöntemler ve İşlevler Kullanılır				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149456				0	tr	\<bookmark_value\>procedures\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>functions;using\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>variables;passing to procedures and functions\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>parameters;for procedures and functions\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>parameters;passing by reference or value\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>variables;scope\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>scope of variables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>GLOBAL variables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>PUBLIC variables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>PRIVATE variables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>functions;return value type\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>return value type of functions\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149456	1			0	tr	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01020300.xhp\"\>Using Procedures and Functions\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150767	2			0	tr	Aşağıdaki açıklama $[officename] Basic'de yöntem ve işlevlerin temel kullanımı ile ilgili açıklamalar içerir.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3151215	56			0	tr	When you create a new module, $[officename] Basic automatically inserts a SUB called "Main". This default name has nothing to do with the order or the starting point of a $[officename] Basic project. You can also safely rename this SUB.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id314756320				0	tr	Some restrictions apply for the names of your public variables, subs, and functions. You must not use the same name as one of the modules of the same library.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3154124	3			0	tr	Procedures (SUBS) and functions (FUNCTIONS) help you maintaining a structured overview by separating a program into logical pieces.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3153193	4			0	tr	One benefit of procedures and functions is that, once you have developed a program code containing task components, you can use this code in another project.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153770	26			0	tr	Passing Variables to Procedures (SUB) and Functions (FUNCTION)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3155414	27			0	tr	Variables can be passed to both procedures and functions. The SUB or FUNCTION must be declared to expect parameters:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3163710	28			0	tr	SUB SubName(\<emph\>Parameter1 As Type, Parameter2 As Type,...\</emph\>)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3151114	29			0	tr	Program kodu				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3146975	30			0	tr	END SUB				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3152577	31			0	tr	The SUB is called using the following syntax:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3159154	32			0	tr	SubName(Value1, Value2,...)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3147124	33			0	tr	The parameters passed to a SUB must fit to those specified in the SUB declaration.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3147397	34			0	tr	The same process applies to FUNCTIONS. In addition, functions always return a function result. The result of a function is defined by assigning the return value to the function name:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3149412	35			0	tr	FUNCTION FunctionName(Parameter1 As Type, Parameter2 As Type,...) As Type				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3156284	36			0	tr	Program kodu				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3145799	37			0	tr	\<emph\>FunctionName=Result\</emph\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150716	38			0	tr	End Function				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3153839	39			0	tr	The FUNCTION is called using the following syntax:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3146914	40			0	tr	Variable=FunctionName(Parameter1, Parameter2,...)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_idN107B3				0	tr	You can also use the fully qualified name to call a procedure or function:\<br/\>\<item type=\"literal\"\>Library.Module.Macro()\</item\>\<br/\> For example, to call the Autotext macro from the Gimmicks library, use the following command:\<br/\>\<item type=\"literal\"\>Gimmicks.AutoText.Main()\</item\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156276	45			0	tr	Passing Variables by Value or Reference				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3155765	47			0	tr	Parameters can be passed to a SUB or a FUNCTION either by reference or by value. Unless otherwise specified, a parameter is always passed by reference. That means that a SUB or a FUNCTION gets the parameter and can read and modify its value.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3145640	53			0	tr	If you want to pass a parameter by value insert the key word "ByVal" in front of the parameter when you call a SUB or FUNCTION, for example:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150042	54			0	tr	Result = Function(\<emph\>ByVal\</emph\> Parameter)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3149258	55			0	tr	In this case, the original content of the parameter will not be modified by the FUNCTION since it only gets the value and not the parameter itself.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150982	57			0	tr	Scope of Variables				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3149814	58			0	tr	A variable defined within a SUB or FUNCTION, only remains valid until the procedure is exited. This is known as a "local" variable. In many cases, you need a variable to be valid in all procedures, in every module of all libraries, or after a SUB or FUNCTION is exited.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154186	59			0	tr	Declaring Variables Outside a SUB or FUNCTION				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150208	111			0	tr	GLOBAL VarName As TYPENAME				20130618 17:22:18
