helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\layers.xhp	0	help	tit				0	tr	About Layers				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\layers.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149018				0	tr	\<bookmark_value\>layers; definition\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\layers.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149018	9			0	tr	\<variable id=\"layers\"\>\<link href=\"text/simpress/guide/layers.xhp\" name=\"About Layers\"\>About Layers\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\layers.xhp	0	help	par_id3146313	3			0	tr	Layers are available in $[officename] Draw, not in $[officename] Impress. Layers allow you to assemble elements on a page that are related. Think of layers as individual workspaces that you can hide from view, hide from printing, or lock. 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\layers.xhp	0	help	par_id7036957				0	tr	Layers do not determine the stacking order of objects on your page, except for the \<emph\>Controls\</emph\> layer which is always in front of other layers. 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\layers.xhp	0	help	par_id1614734				0	tr	The stacking order of objects on your page is determined by the sequence in which you add the objects. You can rearrange the stacking order by \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Modify - Arrange\</item\>. 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\layers.xhp	0	help	par_id398876				0	tr	The areas on a layer that do not contain objects are transparent.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\layers.xhp	0	help	par_id3146962	12			0	tr	$[officename] Draw provides three default layers:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\layers.xhp	0	help	par_id3153073	13			0	tr	Yerleşim				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\layers.xhp	0	help	par_id3149053	14			0	tr	Denetimler				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\layers.xhp	0	help	par_id3150391	15			0	tr	Ölçme çizgileri				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\layers.xhp	0	help	par_id3156397	16			0	tr	You cannot delete or rename the default layers. You can add your own layers by \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Insert - Layer\</item\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\layers.xhp	0	help	par_id3150534	17			0	tr	The \<emph\>Layout\</emph\> layer is the default workspace. The \<emph\>Layout\</emph\> layer determines the location of title, text, and object placeholders on your page.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\layers.xhp	0	help	par_id3150742	18			0	tr	The \<emph\>Controls\</emph\> layer can be used for buttons that have been assigned an action, but that should not be printed. Set the layer's properties to not printable. Objects on the \<emph\>Controls\</emph\> layer are always in front of objects on other layers.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\layers.xhp	0	help	par_id3153085	19			0	tr	The \<emph\>Dimension Lines\</emph\> layer is where you draw, for example, the dimension lines. By switching the layer to show or hide, you can easily switch these lines on and off.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\layers.xhp	0	help	par_id3154507	2			0	tr	You can lock a layer to protect its contents, or hide a layer and its contents from view or from printing. When you add a new layer to a page, the layer is added to all of the pages in your document. However, when you add an object to a layer, it is only added to the current page. If you want the object to appear on all of the pages, add the object to the master page (\<item type=\"menuitem\"\>View - Master\</item\>).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_arrow_styles.xhp	0	help	tit				0	tr	Loading Line and Arrow Styles				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_arrow_styles.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145253				0	tr	\<bookmark_value\>line styles;loading\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>lines;about line ends\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>arrows;loading arrow styles\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>styles;arrow and line styles\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>loading;arrow and line styles\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_arrow_styles.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145253	48			0	tr	\<variable id=\"line_arrow_styles\"\>\<link href=\"text/simpress/guide/line_arrow_styles.xhp\" name=\"Loading Line and Arrow Styles\"\>Loading Line and Arrow Styles\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_arrow_styles.xhp	0	help	par_id3154017	49			0	tr	You can use styles to organize similar line and arrow types. $[officename] provides a few standard style files that you can load and use in your document. If you want, you can add or delete elements from a style file, or even create a custom style file.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_arrow_styles.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154485	50			0	tr	To load a line styles file:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_arrow_styles.xhp	0	help	par_id3156382	58			0	tr	Choose \<emph\>Format - Line\</emph\>, and then click the \<emph\>Line Styles\</emph\> tab.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_arrow_styles.xhp	0	help	par_id3154705	51			0	tr	Click the \<emph\>Load Line Styles\</emph\> button.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_arrow_styles.xhp	0	help	par_id3145588	52			0	tr	Locate the file containing the line styles that you want to load, and then click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>. The file has the format [filename].sod.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_arrow_styles.xhp	0	help	par_id3151240	53			0	tr	To save a line styles file, click the \<emph\>Save Line Styles\</emph\> button, enter a filename, and then click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_arrow_styles.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154765	54			0	tr	To load an arrow styles file:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_arrow_styles.xhp	0	help	par_id3153070	59			0	tr	Choose \<emph\>Format - Line\</emph\>, and then click the \<emph\>Arrow Styles\</emph\> tab.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_arrow_styles.xhp	0	help	par_id3149054	55			0	tr	Click the \<emph\>Load Arrow Styles\</emph\> button.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_arrow_styles.xhp	0	help	par_id3150391	56			0	tr	Locate the file containing the arrow styles that you want to load, and then click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>. The file has the format [filename].soe.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_arrow_styles.xhp	0	help	par_id3166465	57			0	tr	To save an arrow styles file, click the \<emph\>Save Arrow Styles\</emph\> button, enter a filename, and then click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_arrow_styles.xhp	0	help	par_id3145822				0	tr	\<link href=\"text/shared/01/05200000.xhp\" name=\"Format - Line\"\>Format - Line\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_draw.xhp	0	help	tit				0	tr	Drawing Curves				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_draw.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149377				0	tr	\<bookmark_value\>lines; drawing\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>curves; drawing\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>control points definition\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>corner points\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>drawing;lines\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_draw.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149377	3			0	tr	\<variable id=\"line_draw\"\>\<link href=\"text/simpress/guide/line_draw.xhp\" name=\"Drawing Curves\"\>Drawing Curves\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_draw.xhp	0	help	par_id3148868	4			0	tr	The \<emph\>Curve\</emph\> icon \<image id=\"img_id3150205\" src=\"res/commandimagelist/sc_linetoolbox.png\" width=\"0.222inch\" height=\"0.222inch\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3150205\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\> on the \<emph\>Drawing\</emph\> toolbar opens a toolbar to draw Bézier curves. Bézier curves are defined by a start point and an end point, which are called "anchors". The curvature of the Bézier curve is defined by control points ("handles"). Moving a control point changes the shape of the Bézier curve.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_draw.xhp	0	help	par_id8532364				0	tr	Control points are only visible in "Edit Points" mode. Control points are represented by circles, anchor points are represented by squares. The start point is a little bit larger than the other anchor points.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_draw.xhp	0	help	par_id3150210	39			0	tr	Bézier curve segments and straight line segments can be joined to form more complex Bézier curves. Three different transitions can be applied to join adjacent segments:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_draw.xhp	0	help	par_id3154766	25			0	tr	A \<emph\>symmetrical\</emph\> anchor point has the same line curvature on either side, and two control lines that move together as a straight line.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_draw.xhp	0	help	par_id3149874	26			0	tr	A \<emph\>smooth\</emph\> anchor point may have different line curvatures on either side.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_draw.xhp	0	help	par_id3150435	27			0	tr	A \<emph\>corner\</emph\> anchor point has one or two independent control lines. Changing one side has no effect on the other side.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_draw.xhp	0	help	hd_id46388				0	tr	How to use the Curve tool				20130618 17:22:18
