helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id8579363				0	tr	F7				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3227942				0	tr	Add Table or Query				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154939	7			0	tr	Gözat				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148799	8			0	tr	When you open the query design for the first time, in order to create a new query, you can click \<link href=\"text/shared/02/14020100.xhp\" name=\"Add Tables\"\>\<emph\>Add Tables\</emph\>\</link\>. You then see a dialog in which you must first select the table that will be the basis for the query.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3144762	10			0	tr	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_CTL_QRYDGNTAB\"\>Double-click fields to add them to the query. Drag-and-drop to define relations.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157894	141			0	tr	While designing a query, you cannot modify the selected tables.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149562	11			0	tr	Remove tables				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150685	12			0	tr	To remove the table from Design View, click the upper border of the table window and display the context menu. You can use the \<emph\>Delete\</emph\> command to remove the table from the Design View. Another option is to press the Delete key.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150012	142			0	tr	Move table and modify table size				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146922	143			0	tr	You can resize and arrange the tables according to your preferences. To move tables, drag the upper border to the desired position. Enlarge or reduce the size in which the table is displayed by positioning the mouse cursor on a border or on a corner and dragging the table until it is the desired size.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145365	13			0	tr	Table Relations				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154145	14			0	tr	If there are data relations between a field name in one table and a field name in another table, you can use these relations for your query.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152577	15			0	tr	If, for example, you have a spreadsheet for articles identified by an article number, and a spreadsheet for customers in which you record all articles that a customer orders using the corresponding article numbers, then there is a relationship between the two "article number" data fields. If you now want to create a query that returns all articles that a customer has ordered, you must retrieve data from two spreadsheets. To do this, you must tell $[officename] what the relationship exists between the data in the two spreadsheets.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155302	16			0	tr	To do this, click a field name in a table (for example, the field name "Item-Number" from the Customer table), hold down the mouse button and then drag the field name to the field name of the other table ("Item-Number" from the Item table). When you release the mouse button, a line connecting the two fields in the two windows appears. The corresponding condition that the content of the two field names must be identical is entered in the resulting SQL query.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153876	137			0	tr	The creation of a query that is based on several related sheets is only possible if you use $[officename] as the interface for a relational database.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145646	246			0	tr	You cannot access tables from different databases in a query. Queries involving multiple tables can only be created within one database.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153279	224			0	tr	Specifying link type				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154791	225			0	tr	If you double-click the line connecting two linked fields or call the menu command \<emph\>Insert - New Relation\</emph\>, you can specify the type of link in the \<link href=\"text/shared/explorer/database/02010101.xhp\" name=\"Relations\"\>\<emph\>Relations\</emph\>\</link\> dialog.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150094	285			0	tr	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QUERY_EDIT_JOINCONNECTION\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Edit Join Properties.\</ahelp\>Alternatively, press Tab until the line is selected, then press Shift+F10 to display the context menu and there choose the command \<emph\>Edit\</emph\>. Some databases support only a subset of the possible join types.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155851	145			0	tr	Deleting relations				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156178	146			0	tr	To delete a relation between two tables, click the connection line and then press the Delete key.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150715	284			0	tr	Alternatively, delete the respective entries in \<emph\>Fields involved \</emph\>in the \<emph\>Relations\</emph\> dialog. Or press Tab until the connecting vector is displayed highlighted, then press Shift+F10 to open the context menu and select \<emph\>Delete \</emph\>command.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151208	17			0	tr	Define query				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3158416	18			0	tr	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_CTL_QRYDGNCRIT\"\>Select conditions to define the query.\</ahelp\> Each column of the design table accepts a data field for the query. The conditions in one row are linked with a Boolean AND.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154161	19			0	tr	Specify field name				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146791	20			0	tr	First, select all field names from the tables that you want to add to the query. You can do this either with drag-and-drop or by double-clicking a field name in the table window. With the drag-and-drop method, use the mouse to drag a field name from the table window into the lower area of the query design. As you do this, you can decide which column you want to add the field to. Select a field name by double-clicking. It will then be added to the next free column.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150750	21			0	tr	Deleting field names				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154479	22			0	tr	To remove a field name from the query, click the column header of the field and choose the \<emph\>Delete\</emph\> command on the context menu for the column.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155764	277			0	tr	Sorguyu kaydet				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148481	278			0	tr	Use the \<emph\>Save\</emph\> icon on the Standard Bar to save the query. You see a dialog that asks you to enter a name for the query. If the database supports schemas, you can also enter a schema.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154362	279			0	tr	Schema				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154754	280			0	tr	\<ahelp hid=\"DBACCESS_COMBOBOX_DLG_SAVE_AS_ET_SCHEMA\"\>Enter the name of the schema that is assigned to the query or table view.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156717	281			0	tr	Query name or table view name				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154253	282			0	tr	\<ahelp hid=\"DBACCESS_EDIT_DLG_SAVE_AS_ET_TITLE\"\>Enter the name of the query or table view.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3163805	23			0	tr	Filtering data				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154964	24			0	tr	To filter data for the query, set the desired preferences in the lower area of the Design View. The following lines are available:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146916	25			0	tr	Alan				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156372	26			0	tr	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_FIELD\"\>Enter the name of the data field that you referred to in the Query. All settings made in the lower rows refer to this field.\</ahelp\> If you activate a cell with a mouse click you'll see an arrow button, which enables you to select a field. The "Table name.*" option selects all data fields and the criteria is valid for all table fields.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145150	148			0	tr	Takma Ad				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146315	149			0	tr	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_ALIAS\"\>Specifies an alias. This alias will be listed in a query instead of the field name. This makes it possible to use user-defined column labels.\</ahelp\> For example, if the data field has the name PtNo and, instead of that name, you would like to have PartNum appear in the query, enter PartNum as alias.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155959	193			0	tr	In an SQL statement, aliases are defined as following:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149922	194			0	tr	SELECT column AS alias FROM table.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3159335	195			0	tr	Örneğin:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148478	196			0	tr	SELECT "PtNo" AS "PartNum" FROM "Parts"				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148485	27			0	tr	Tablo				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3163665	28			0	tr	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_TABLE\"\>The corresponding database table of the selected data field is listed here.\</ahelp\> If you activate the a cell with a mouse click, an arrow will appear which enables you to select another table of the current query.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154207	29			0	tr	Sırala				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150979	30			0	tr	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_ORDER\"\>If you click the cell, you can select among the sorting options: ascending, descending and not sorted.\</ahelp\> Text fields will be sorted alphabetically and numerical fields numerically. For most databases, administrators can set the sorting options.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150384	31			0	tr	Visible				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146133	32			0	tr	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_VISIBLE\"\>If you mark the \<emph\>Visible\</emph\> property for a data field, that field will be visible in the query\</ahelp\>. If you only use a data field to formulate a condition, you do not necessarily need to show it.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154714	33			0	tr	Ölçüt				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145134	34			0	tr	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_CRIT\"\>Specifies the \<link href=\"text/shared/explorer/database/02010100.xhp\" name=\"criteria \"\>criteria \</link\>by which the content of the data field should be filtered.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152477	35			0	tr	VEYA				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154585	36			0	tr	Here you can enter one additional criterion for filtering in each line. Multiple criteria in one column will be connected by an OR link.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148800	150			0	tr	You can also use the context menu of the line headers in the lower area of the query design to insert another line for functions:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148419	151			0	tr	İşlevler				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153233	152			0	tr	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_FUNCTION\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Select a function to run in the query here.\</ahelp\> The functions you can run here depend on the database. 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id8760818				0	tr	If you are working with the HSQL database, the list box in the \<emph\>Function\</emph\> row offers you the following options:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150307	179			0	tr	Seçenek				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3166430	180			0	tr	SQL				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152993	181			0	tr	Efekt				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155377	162			0	tr	İşlev yok				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155533	163			0	tr	No function will be executed.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3166420	164			0	tr	Ortalama 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145268	183			0	tr	AVG				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154486	165			0	tr	Calculates the arithmetic mean of a field.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149979	166			0	tr	Say				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154260	184			0	tr	BAĞ_DEĞ_SAY				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155810	167			0	tr	Determines the number of records in the table. Empty fields can either be counted (a) or not (b).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151333	197			0	tr	a) COUNT(*): Passing an asterisk as the argument counts all records in the table.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152889	198			0	tr	b) COUNT(column): Passing a field name as an argument counts only fields in which the field name in question contains a value. Null values (empty fields) will not be counted.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153067	168			0	tr	Azami				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148840	185			0	tr	AZAMİ				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3159221	169			0	tr	Determines the highest value of a field.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146866	170			0	tr	Asgari				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148604	186			0	tr	MİN				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157982	171			0	tr	Determines the lowest value of a field.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154828	172			0	tr	Toplam				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147070	187			0	tr	TOPLAM				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154536	173			0	tr	Calculates the sum of values of associated fields.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148820	174			0	tr	Gruplama				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145375	188			0	tr	GROUP BY				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149438	175			0	tr	Groups query data according to the field name selected. Functions are executed according to the specified groups. In SQL, this option corresponds to the GROUP BY clause. If a criterion is added, this entry appears in the SQL HAVING.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156038	189			0	tr	You can also enter function calls directly into the SQL statement. The syntax is:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156340	190			0	tr	SELECT FUNCTION(column) FROM table.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155075	191			0	tr	For example, the function call in SQL for calculating a sum is:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154591	192			0	tr	SELECT SUM("Price") FROM "Article".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3159205	176			0	tr	Except for the \<emph\>Group\</emph\> function, the above functions are so-called Aggregate functions. These are functions that calculate data to create summaries from the results. Additional functions that are not listed in the list box might be also possible. These depend on the specific database system in use and on the current state of the Base driver.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148651	177			0	tr	To use other functions not listed in the list box, you must enter them under \<emph\>Field\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155098	178			0	tr	You can also assign aliases to function calls. If the query is not to be displayed in the column header, enter the desired name under \<emph\>Alias\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155539	199			0	tr	The corresponding function in an SQL statement is:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149425	200			0	tr	SELECT FUNCTION() AS alias FROM table				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3144431	201			0	tr	Örnek:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154614	202			0	tr	SELECT COUNT(*) AS count FROM "Item"				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154610	203			0	tr	If you run this function, you cannot insert any additional columns for the query other than receiving these columns as a "Group" function.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154644	204			0	tr	\<emph\>Examples\</emph\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151120	205			0	tr	In the following example, a query is run through two tables: an "Item" table with the "Item_No" field and a "Suppliers" table with the "Supplier_Name" field. In addition, both tables have a common field name "Supplier_No."				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155144	206			0	tr	The following steps are required to create a query containing all suppliers who deliver more than three items.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153240	207			0	tr	Insert the "Item" and "Suppliers" tables into the query design.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148807	208			0	tr	Link the "Supplier_No" fields of the two tables if there is not already a relation of this type.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3161652	209			0	tr	Double-click the "Item_No" field from the "Item" table. Display the \<emph\>Function\</emph\> line using the context menu and select the Count function.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151009	210			0	tr	Enter >3 as a criterion and disable the Visible field.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145601	211			0	tr	Double-click the "Supplier_Name" field in the "Suppliers" table and choose the Group function.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147512	212			0	tr	Run the query.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148638	213			0	tr	If the "price" (for the individual price of an article) and "Supplier_No" (for the supplier of the article) fields exist in the "Item" table, you can obtain the average price of the item that a supplier provides with the following query:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153045	214			0	tr	Insert the "Item" table into the query design.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149802	215			0	tr	Double-click the "Price" and "Supplier_No" fields.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153554	216			0	tr	Enable the \<emph\>Function\</emph\> line and select the Average function from the "Price" field.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155597	217			0	tr	You can also enter "Average" in the line for the alias name (without quotation marks).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151191	218			0	tr	Choose Group for the "Supplier_No" field.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155547	219			0	tr	Run the query.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147549	247			0	tr	The following context menu commands and symbols are available:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154172	248			0	tr	İşlevler				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150414	249			0	tr	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_FUNCTION\"\>Shows or hides a row for selection of functions.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149872	153			0	tr	Tablo İsmi				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147246	154			0	tr	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_FUNCTION\"\>Shows or hides the row for the table name.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145117	155			0	tr	Alias Name				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155754	156			0	tr	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_FUNCTION\"\>Shows or hides the row for the alias name.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153298	157			0	tr	Birbirinden Farklı Değerler				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147500	158			0	tr	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_FUNCTION\"\>Applies only distinct values to the query.\</ahelp\> This applies to records containing data that appears several times in the selected fields. If the \<emph\>Distinct Values\</emph\> command is active, you will see only one record in the query (DISTINCT). Otherwise, you will see all records corresponding to the query criteria (ALL).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150436	159			0	tr	For example, if the name "Smith" occurs several times in your address database, you can choose the\<emph\> Distinct Values\</emph\> command to specify in the query that the name "Smith" will occur only once.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152352	160			0	tr	For a query involving several fields, the combination of values from all fields must be unique so that the result can be formed from a specific record. For example, you have "Smith in Chicago" once in your address book and "Smith in London" twice. With the\<emph\> Distinct Values\</emph\> command, the query will use the two fields "last name" and "city" and return the query result "Smith in Chicago" once and "Smith in London" once.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149825	161			0	tr	In SQL, this command corresponds to the DISTINCT predicate.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148926	37			0	tr	Formulating filter conditions				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153162	38			0	tr	When formulating filter conditions, various operators and commands are available to you. Apart from the relational operators, there are SQL-specific commands that query the content of database fields. If you use these commands in the $[officename] syntax, $[officename] automatically converts these into the corresponding SQL syntax. You can also enter the SQL command directly. The following tables give an overview of the operators and commands:				20130618 17:22:18
